The code is clean — no custom `listRowBackground` overrides anywhere. The only remaining fix is adding `.foregroundStyle(.primary)` to section header labels so macOS's sidebar list doesn't apply its default secondary/tertiary tint to `DisclosureGroup` labels. Making the change now.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
In `List(selection:)` with `.listStyle(.sidebar)`, SwiftUI on macOS applies `.secondary` foreground tinting to `DisclosureGroup` labels by default — treating them as non-interactive headers. Explicitly setting `.foregroundStyle(.primary)` overrides this, giving the bold-but-dark appearance of apps like Reeder and the SimpleSidebar reference. The `.selectionDisabled()` modifier on the `Text` is correctly placed inside the label closure — it marks the header row as non-selectable in the list's selection model.
